From bf20a4ec57ba8b2353315e9436eb16f5b241b14f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Prism <prism@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 23:01:17 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] [Win] Video frames straight to the GPU process's compositor

Without coordinated graphics, every video frame was painted on the web
process's main thread (converted to a NativeImage, recorded in a display list
and rasterized in the GPU process), so video stuttered whenever the page was
busy and topped out at ~20-25 fps at 1080p.

Now, on the WC compositor (Windows), MediaPlayerPrivateGStreamer gets a
WCVideoFrameSink: the video's layer is composited, and the streaming thread
copies each new frame into shared memory and sends it to the GPU process
(GPUConnectionToWebProcess::PushWCVideoFrame). There WCVideoLayerManager keeps
the newest frame per video as a TextureMapperPlatformLayer, uploads it when
painted, and recomposites the scenes showing it right away (WCScene::
recomposite()), without waiting for the page's next rendering update.
WCLayerUpdateInfo carries the video layer's identifier.

Pages rendered offscreen fall back to painting (their scenes only reach the
screen with an update), and WEBKIT_WC_VIDEO_LAYER=0 turns it off.
